00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.508
MARK MIRCHANDANI: What's
Google Cloud Composer?

00:00:11.508 --> 00:00:13.194
MICHEL SZARINDAR:
Ah, let me show you.

00:00:13.194 --> 00:00:15.584
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.761 --> 00:00:21.260
MICHEL SZARINDAR: So let's say
that you have a data workflow

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.690
to collect, to move,
to transform, and check

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:24.920
these data.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.810
And all these tasks
needs to be finished

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.180
at the same time every day.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.820
So you need to make sure that
you can schedule, monitor,

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:34.850
restart, and even chain
different tasks together.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.790
Google Cloud Composer is
a fully-managed version

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:41.057
of Apache Airflow and
will let you do all this.

00:00:41.057 --> 00:00:43.640
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So for anyone
already familiar with Airflow,

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:44.660
it's like the same
thing, but you

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.580
don't have to worry about
managing the infrastructure.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:47.280
MICHEL SZARINDAR: Exactly, yeah.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.140
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Oh,
that sounds really useful.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:49.515
Can we look more
at your example?

00:00:49.515 --> 00:00:50.830
MICHEL SZARINDAR: Yeah.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:53.330
In this architecture,
for example, the workflow

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:56.130
will move data from table
in BigQuery in the US

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.060
to BigQuery in the EU.

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.730
To do that, we are
exporting and transferring

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:05.459
data between two different
Google Cloud Storage buckets.

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:07.145
Let's jump to the interface.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.790
Here is a list of the
environments for Google Cloud

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:13.420
Platform.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.640
And I'm going to show you how
easy it is to create a new one.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.260
So here, you are adding the
name of a new environment.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.150
You can just choose
your location--

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.560
us-central1 here-- and
no preference on zone.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.240
There are other
configuration points,

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.140
but I'm not going
to show you this.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.880
So let's do Create.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.250
So while the
environment is creating,

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.980
we are going to see an example.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:42.530
OK, so here you have all the
details of the environment,

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.700
and you also have, for example,
for this one, the work nodes.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.560
You can just check here.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.440
And we are going to go
inside the Airflow UI, which

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.070
is created automatically.

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:56.007
And this is a console where
you store all of your DAGs.

00:01:56.007 --> 00:01:57.090
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So wait.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.440
What is a DAG?

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.590
MICHEL SZARINDAR: A DAG
is a Directed Acyclic

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:02.506
Graph, of course.

00:02:02.506 --> 00:02:05.140
[EXHALES] A DAG is
like a workflow.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:08.830
Think of it as an organized list
of tasks that you want to run.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:11.060
Let me show you that.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.590
Let's look inside the code.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:17.260
So inside the code view, can
see all the code for this DAG.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.180
So here on the bottom, you're
going to see all the tasks.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:21.870
So for example, I start.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:25.530
And after I take the
data from BigQuery,

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.170
I export that to Cloud Storage.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:33.210
From Cloud Storage, I'm going
to move the data from US to EU.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.310
And when it's inside
the EU, I'm going

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:40.680
to start importing back
the data inside BigQ.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.060
So let's go back to the DAG.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.986
So I'm going to start
the DAG just now.

00:02:43.986 --> 00:02:45.360
It's normally done
automatically.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.160
But I'm going to
manually start the DAG.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.710
And there are different
ways to see the DAG running.

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:56.960
So for example, I'm going
to show you the tree view.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.450
And there also a
nice one, which is

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.640
the graph view, where I can
see all the status of all

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:02.600
the tasks.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.890
Here, for example, the task
is soon going to be queued--

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:08.770
yes, here.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:13.430
And the task is soon
going to be started.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:15.940
So one of the beauty, as
well, of Cloud Composer

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:20.290
is that you can just see the
execution of the DAGs day

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.620
after time.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.640
So here, for example, you
have all the landing times

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.230
where you can start looking
at the execution over time.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.550
So now, you can monitor
all your workflows,

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:33.594
and relax, and be sure your
job will be completed on time.

00:03:33.594 --> 00:03:34.510
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Wow.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:35.135
That's awesome.

00:03:35.135 --> 00:03:37.900
Now I'm feeling
much more composed.

